  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Introduction$ih$, $ih$Every ML engineer is first a programmer who is comfortable moving data around. This module builds that foundation: enough Python to be dangerous, NumPy for numerical computation, Pandas for tabular data, and the vocabulary of machine learning itself.

Nothing here is decorative — every tool introduced is used again in later modules. You need working fluency, not mastery: variables and types (int, float, str, bool), operators, if/elif/else, for/while loops, functions (including default arguments and *args/**kwargs), lambda functions, list/dict/set comprehensions, exception handling (try/except), file I/O, basic classes (__init__, methods, attributes), modules/imports, virtual environments (venv), and pip for installing packages.

Jupyter Notebook/JupyterLab is the standard environment for exploratory ML work; VS Code is used for production-grade scripts.

**Why it matters.** Think of this module as the "toolbox assembly" hour of a woodworking apprenticeship: you will spend the next 10 modules solving machine learning problems, and every single tool you touch here — Python's data structures, NumPy arrays, Pandas frames — is the thing you reach for first on every real project. An ML engineer who cannot manipulate data fluently spends 80% of their time fighting the tools instead of thinking about the problem.

**Step-by-step intuition.** Work through this learning loop: (1) read a short concept, (2) type a 5-line snippet into a notebook yourself — never copy-paste, (3) mutate the snippet and predict the output before running it, (4) explain the output aloud in one sentence. This "predict, run, explain" cycle is the fastest way to convert reading into fluency, and it is exactly how experienced engineers onboard new tools.

**Practitioner notes.** Set up a clean environment now: create a virtual environment, pin your package versions, and install Jupyter. A messy environment is the root cause of "it works on my machine" disasters later in Module 7, and fixing it now costs five minutes instead of five days.$ih$, 20, 1, 1, $ih$Python, Data & Machine Learning Foundations$ih$, true, false, false, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
