  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Pandas — Tabular Data$ih$, $ih$Pandas' two core objects are the Series (1D, labeled) and DataFrame (2D, labeled rows and columns) — think "a spreadsheet with superpowers."

When to use .loc vs .iloc: .loc selects by label (df.loc[3, "age"]), .iloc selects by integer position (df.iloc[0, 1]). Mixing them up silently returns the wrong row when the index isn't a simple 0..n range — a classic source of quiet bugs.

**Why it matters.** A DataFrame is the table you will live inside. Every real dataset arrives as rows and columns, and the difference between a competent analyst and a frustrated one is whether they can filter, group, and join with two or three keystrokes of idiomatic Pandas. Analogy: think of the DataFrame as a spreadsheet that never forgets its column names — and .loc/.iloc as the two ways to point at cells: by the name tag (.loc) or by the seat number (.iloc).

**Step-by-step intuition.** (1) df[boolean_mask] selects rows where the mask is True — df[df.age > 30] is "rows where age > 30". (2) df.groupby("city")["income"].mean() reads as "split by city, take income, average it" — three verbs, one line. (3) A merge is a lookup: how = "left" means "keep every row of the left table, attach matching values from the right, fill missing with NaN". (4) isna().sum() is the missing-data census you run before any modeling, because most models cannot handle NaN at all.

**Practitioner notes.** Use .loc for assignments (df.loc[df.age > 30, "segment"] = "senior") to avoid the SettingWithCopyWarning that silently corrupts data. Check df.info() first, every time — dtype and non-null counts catch 50% of all data bugs before they reach a model.

**Practice: run this example**

```
import pandas as pd

df = pd.read_csv("customers.csv")
df.head()                                        # first 5 rows
df.info()                                        # dtypes, non-null counts
df["age"]                                        # select a column (Series)
df[df["age"] > 30]                               # filter rows
df.sort_values("income", ascending=False)        # sort
df.groupby("city")["income"].mean()              # aggregate by group
df.merge(orders_df, on="customer_id", how="left")  # join tables
df.isna().sum()                                  # count missing values per column

import pandas as pd
import numpy as np

df = pd.DataFrame({
    "customer_id": [101, 102, 103, 104, 105],
    "city": ["Karachi", "Lahore", "Karachi", "Islamabad", "Lahore"],
    "age": [25, 41, np.nan, 33, 29],
    "income": [30000, 82000, 45000, 60000, 51000],
})

# 1) Inspect first, model later
print(df.info())                 # dtypes + non-null counts (age has NaN!)
print(df.isna().sum())           # age: 1 missing value

# 2) Filter rows with a boolean mask
seniors = df[df["age"] > 30]

# 3) .loc: select by label AND assign safely
df.loc[df["age"] > 30, "segment"] = "senior"
df.loc[df["age"] <= 30, "segment"] = "young"

# 4) Group + aggregate: average income per city
by_city = df.groupby("city")["income"].mean().round(0)

# 5) Sort and take the top rows
top = df.sort_values("income", ascending=False).head(2)

# 6) Merge: attach order data to customers (left join)
orders = pd.DataFrame({
    "customer_id": [101, 101, 103],
    "order_total": [120, 340, 75],
})
merged = df.merge(orders, on="customer_id", how="left")

# 7) Fill missing values (do this AFTER inspection, never blindly)
df["age"] = df["age"].fillna(df["age"].median())
print(df.head())
```
$ih$, 20, 5, 1, $ih$Python, Data & Machine Learning Foundations$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$code$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$What Is Machine Learning?$ih$, $ih$Artificial Intelligence (AI) is the broad goal of building systems that perform tasks requiring intelligence. Machine Learning (ML) is a subset of AI where a system learns patterns from data rather than following hand-written rules. Deep Learning is a subset of ML using multi-layer neural networks, particularly effective on unstructured data (images, text, audio).

- Supervised learning: learn a mapping from inputs (features) to known outputs (labels/targets), e.g., predicting house price from square footage.
- Unsupervised learning: find structure in data with no labels, e.g., grouping customers into segments.
- Reinforcement learning: an agent learns by taking actions in an environment and receiving rewards.

Vocabulary you must know cold: a sample (one row/observation), features (input columns), label/target (what you're predicting), a model (the learned function mapping features → target), training (fitting the model to data), validation (tuning decisions on held-out data), testing (final, untouched performance check), inference (using a trained model on new data).

**Why it matters.** The word "learning" is the most misleading word in our field: a model does not understand anything — it records statistical regularities between numbers. Analogy: ML is like teaching someone to recognize birds by showing them 10,000 labeled photos; the learner never learns "what a bird is", only "which pixel patterns tend to co-occur with the bird label".

**Step-by-step intuition.** Induction is the core idea: from specific examples (training data) the model infers a general rule (a function from features to labels). The reason we split data into train/validation/test is that we must verify the rule generalizes to examples the model never saw — otherwise you are measuring memorization, not learning. Supervised = the teacher's answer key exists (labels). Unsupervised = no answer key, the algorithm hunts for structure. Reinforcement = learning by trial, error, and reward signals over time.

**Practitioner notes.** Internalize the vocabulary until it is reflex: sample, feature, label, model, training, validation, testing, inference. These eight words are the entire field in miniature — every advanced concept (cross-validation, overfitting, data leakage, deployment) is a variation on one of them.$ih$, 20, 6, 1, $ih$Python, Data & Machine Learning Foundations$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$The ML Workflow$ih$, $ih$Raw Data → Preprocessing → Train/Val/Test Split → Training → Validation (tune) → Testing (final check) → Deployment / Inference.

Every module after this one deepens one box in that pipeline. Module 2 is Preprocessing. Modules 3-4 are Training (regression, classification). Module 5 is Validation/Testing done rigorously. Module 10 is Deployment.

**Why it matters.** The workflow is the map of the whole course. Every time you get lost in a later module — "why is my validation score crazy?" — the answer lives in one of these boxes: a preprocessing step ran after the split (leakage), tuning happened on the test set (contamination), or deployment skipped the validation box entirely. Analogy: it is a kitchen pipeline — you prep ingredients (preprocess), portion them (split), cook on the training portion, taste-test on the validation portion, and only serve the dish (deploy) after a final quality check on the untouched test portion.

**Step-by-step intuition.** The critical ordering rule: anything that inspects data to make decisions (imputation medians, scaler means, feature-selection fits) must be fit on the training split ONLY, then applied to validation/test. If the scaler sees the test data before the test is run, the test is no longer a test — the model has effectively peeked at the answer sheet. This one rule is the single most expensive lesson to learn the hard way.

**Practitioner notes.** Expect the workflow to be iterative, not linear: when validation performance disappoints, you do not restart — you return to preprocessing or feature engineering and re-run the loop. Professional teams automate this loop with pipelines (Module 5) so the ordering rules cannot be violated by hand.$ih$, 20, 7, 1, $ih$Python, Data & Machine Learning Foundations$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Real-World Example$ih$, $ih$A streaming service predicting "will this user cancel next month?" starts exactly here: raw event logs and billing records (Pandas), turned into numeric arrays (NumPy), fed into a supervised classification model (Module 4) — churn (yes/no) is the label, watch-time/payment-history are the features.

**Why it matters.** Churn prediction is the classic "first real project" because it is a pure, self-contained supervised problem: you have historical data, a clear binary label (canceled or not), and a business metric (revenue saved). Walking the example through the workflow end-to-end now gives you a template you can map onto any future dataset.

**Step-by-step intuition.** Follow the boxes: (1) Raw data — event logs and billing records arrive as messy tables; (2) Pandas — you load them, join on customer_id, and compute derived features like "months since last payment" or "support tickets in 30 days"; (3) NumPy — every feature becomes a numeric array and missing values become NaN to handle; (4) the label is "churned" (1) vs "active" (0) measured a fixed window after the features; (5) a classifier from Module 4 learns P(churn | features); (6) validation answers "is the model better than just predicting the majority class?"; (7) deployment scores each customer monthly and the business targets the top-decile risk group.

**Practitioner notes.** Notice the design decisions hidden in that walkthrough: the definition of "churned", the feature window, and the deployment threshold are all business decisions you must make explicitly and document — they are where a data scientist's real judgment lives.$ih$, 20, 8, 1, $ih$Python, Data & Machine Learning Foundations$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Common Mistakes$ih$, $ih$- Confusing = (assignment) with == (comparison) in conditions.
- Forgetting that Pandas filtering (df[df.age > 30]) returns a copy by default, causing SettingWithCopyWarning on later edits.
- Assuming a model "understands" data the way a human does — it only ever sees numbers.

**Why it matters.** The mistakes in this lesson are the "serial killers" of ML code — quiet, common, and data-corrupting. None of them raises an error; they just produce subtly wrong results that surface as confusing model behavior days later.

**Step-by-step intuition.** Walk through each one: (1) = vs == — assignment mutates a variable, comparison tests it; an if condition that assigns instead of compares evaluates a truthy value and silently always runs. (2) Chained assignment like df[df.age > 30]["income"] = 0 triggers Pandas' SettingWithCopyWarning because the intermediate selection can be a copy — the write lands nowhere, your data stays unmodified, and you may not notice. (3) NaN poisoning: np.nan != np.nan is True, so missing values never match themselves; always use pd.isna() or .dropna(). (4) The model-sees-numbers myth: passing text into a model that expects numbers will either crash or coerce silently (strings become object dtype), so encoding is a mandatory step, not an option.

**Practitioner notes.** Cultivate the habit of asserting data invariants: df["age"].isna().sum() == 0, X.shape[0] == y.shape[0], and df.dtypes after every preprocessing step. Three one-line asserts in the morning prevent three all-day debugging sessions.$ih$, 20, 9, 1, $ih$Python, Data & Machine Learning Foundations$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Professional Tip$ih$, $ih$Set a random seed (np.random.default_rng(42)) at the start of every project. Reproducibility is a professional habit, not an academic nicety — you cannot debug or compare models you cannot reproduce.

**Why it matters.** Professionals are not faster because they know more commands — they are faster because their environment and habits remove failure points. Analogy: a surgeon's scrub routine looks like ceremony until you realize it is a checklist that eliminates a class of disasters.

**Step-by-step intuition.** Build your routine now, in this order: (1) every project gets its own virtual environment and a requirements.txt pinned to exact versions (numpy==1.26.4, not numpy>=1.20) — this is what makes "it works on my machine" reproducible on a teammate's machine; (2) notebooks get a clean execution order — keep all imports and data loading in the top cells so you can "Restart and Run All" safely, and never re-run cells out of order after mutating data; (3) seed every random process you control so runs are comparable; (4) write small functions for reusable steps instead of copy-pasting cell bodies — duplication is where fixes fail to propagate.

**Practitioner notes.** The professional habit that pays the most: before every modeling decision, write down the expected outcome ("scaling should not change R-squared for linear regression"). Running experiments against your predictions is how you build calibrated intuition — and it makes you notice anomalies the way experts do.$ih$, 20, 10, 1, $ih$Python, Data & Machine Learning Foundations$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Introduction$ih$, $ih$Real datasets are messy: missing values, duplicates, wrong types, outliers. Before any model can be built, the data must be understood and cleaned.

This module also introduces feature engineering — turning raw columns into signals a model can actually learn from.

**Why it matters.** Data scientists spend roughly 60-80% of their time on data preparation, not modeling. This module is where the "science" in data science happens: converting messy, missing, inconsistent raw data into clean, numeric features a model can learn from. Analogy: a chef can have the best recipes in the world (algorithms), but cooking with spoiled or unmixed ingredients produces inedible food — and a model trained on dirty data produces confident, wrong predictions.

**Step-by-step intuition.** The module follows the real order of work: (1) audit quality — missing values, duplicates, impossible values; (2) describe — summary statistics and distributions; (3) explore — plots and tables that reveal relationships; (4) preprocess — scale, encode, impute; (5) engineer — create new features that encode domain knowledge. Each step feeds the next, and each has canonical mistakes that are covered as you go.

**Practitioner notes.** Rule of thumb: if a step does not change the data visibly or measurably, you have not finished it. Quantify everything — missingness percentages, distributions before and after scaling — because "trust me, I cleaned it" is not a phrase that survives code review.$ih$, 20, 11, 2, $ih$Data Cleaning, EDA & Feature Engineering$ih$, true, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Learning Objectives$ih$, $ih$- Diagnose data quality issues: missing values, duplicates, invalid values, outliers.
- Distinguish numerical, categorical, and ordinal features.
- Compute and interpret mean, median, mode, variance, standard deviation, percentiles, correlation, and covariance.
- Perform exploratory data analysis (EDA) using histograms, box plots, scatter plots, and correlation matrices.
- Apply encoding, scaling, and transformation techniques correctly.
- Explain data leakage and prevent it.

**Why it matters.** The objectives here are the "operational core" of data science — they are the exact skills interview take-home assignments test. Every one of them is a deliverable in real projects: a quality report, an EDA notebook, a preprocessing pipeline, a feature dictionary.

**Step-by-step intuition.** Interpret each objective as a deliverable: "detect and handle missing values and duplicates" means you can produce a missingness table and justify your imputation strategy; "compute descriptive statistics" means you can explain what the mean, median, and standard deviation actually say about a skewed column; "perform EDA" means you can go from a raw CSV to a one-page summary of the data's structure and the relationships a model could exploit. The leakage objective is the one that separates juniors from seniors — most interview horror stories are leakage stories.

**Practitioner notes.** Treat this module's exercises as a checklist for the take-home test you will eventually be given: load → profile → clean → explore → transform → document. If you can do all six without looking anything up, you are ahead of most candidates at the 3-year experience level.$ih$, 20, 12, 2, $ih$Data Cleaning, EDA & Feature Engineering$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Data Quality$ih$, $ih$Data quality issues and their typical fixes:

- Missing values: NaN in "income" → impute (mean/median/mode) or drop.
- Duplicates: same customer row twice → df.drop_duplicates().
- Invalid values: age = -5 → clip, correct, or drop.
- Outliers: income = $50,000,000 → cap, transform, or investigate.
- Wrong dtype: "42" stored as text → cast with astype().

**Why it matters.** Every model inherits the quality of its data, and most data-quality bugs are silent: duplicates inflate training weight, inconsistent strings fragment categories ("NYC", "nyc", "New York" become three features), and impossible values poison statistics. Analogy: data quality is the foundation of a building — you cannot see the cracks once the walls (the model) are up, but the whole structure inherits them.

**Step-by-step intuition.** Missing data has three mechanisms with different remedies: MCAR (missing completely at random — like a sensor that failed occasionally) is safest to drop or impute simply; MAR (missing at random given other features — e.g., high-income customers hide their income) is predictable from other columns, so model-based imputation works; MNAR (missing not at random — e.g., people skip questions they are ashamed of) is dangerous because the very fact of missingness carries signal you may need as its own feature. Check counts and patterns first: df.isna().sum() gives volume, a missingness heatmap gives structure.

**Practitioner notes.** Deduplicate on a key that defines a unique entity, not on all columns — two legitimate rows can share every value. And never impute with statistics computed from the whole dataset before the train/test split: the imputer's median must be learned from training rows only (Module 2.8 covers this).

**Practice: run this example**

```
import pandas as pd

df = pd.read_csv("customers.csv")

# Deduplicate identical rows
df = df.drop_duplicates()

# Clip invalid values into a plausible range
df["age"] = df["age"].clip(0, 120)

# Median imputation for missing numeric values
df["income"].fillna(df["income"].median(), inplace=True)

# Fix wrong dtypes
df["price"] = df["price"].astype(float)

import pandas as pd
import numpy as np

df = pd.DataFrame({
    "user_id": [1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5],
    "city": ["NYC", "nyc", "New York", "LA", "LA", np.nan],
    "income": [50000, 50000, 60000, np.nan, 90000, 75000],
    "age": [30, 30, 25, np.nan, 41, np.nan],
})

# 1) Duplicates: drop rows with the same user_id (entity-level key)
print("before:", len(df))
df = df.drop_duplicates(subset="user_id", keep="first")
print("after:", len(df))

# 2) Standardize category text BEFORE counting categories
df["city"] = df["city"].str.strip().str.title()   # "nyc" -> "Nyc"
df.loc[df["city"] == "Nyc", "city"] = "New York"

# 3) Missingness census: count + percentage per column
miss = pd.DataFrame({
    "count": df.isna().sum(),
    "pct": (df.isna().sum() / len(df) * 100).round(1),
})

# 4) Impossible values: flag, don't delete silently
df["age_outlier"] = (df["age"] > 120) | (df["age"] < 0)

# 5) Simple, defensible imputation: median for skewed numeric columns
df["income"] = df["income"].fillna(df["income"].median())
df["age"] = df["age"].fillna(df["age"].median())

# 6) Keep a missingness indicator when the fact of absence is signal
df["age_was_missing"] = df["age"].isna().astype(int)
print(df.head())
```
$ih$, 20, 13, 2, $ih$Data Cleaning, EDA & Feature Engineering$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$code$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Descriptive Statistics$ih$, $ih$- Mean — arithmetic average; sensitive to outliers.
- Median — middle value; robust to outliers.
- Mode — most frequent value; used for categorical data.
- Variance (σ²) — average squared distance from the mean; standard deviation (σ) is its square root, in the original units.
- Percentiles — the value below which a given % of data falls (the median is the 50th percentile).
- Correlation (Pearson's r, range −1 to 1) measures linear association between two numeric variables.
- Covariance measures joint variability but, unlike correlation, is not scale-independent, so it's harder to interpret directly.

**Why it matters.** Summary statistics are how you "read" a column at a glance — and they determine preprocessing decisions: a highly skewed column may need a log transform, a multi-modal distribution may indicate hidden groups, and the mean vs. median gap tells you whether outliers dominate. Analogy: a single photo (one statistic) cannot summarize a movie (the distribution) — that is why you always look at both numbers and shapes.

**Step-by-step intuition.** (1) Mean = balance point; median = midpoint; when they diverge, skew is present (positive skew pulls the mean above the median). (2) Variance = average squared distance from the mean; standard deviation is its interpretable square root in original units; both punish outliers twice (squaring), so they are sensitive to contamination. (3) Quantiles split the ranked data — the 25th/50th/75th percentiles (quartiles) and the IQR = Q3 - Q1 define the classic boxplot whiskers and the 1.5 x IQR outlier rule. (4) Skewness > ~1 or < -1 signals heavy tails; for log-normal-ish data, log(x + 1) transforms the distribution toward symmetric.

**Practitioner notes.** Always report describe() AND the distribution shape: a column with mean 50 and median 50 can still be bimodal (two peaks at 0 and 100). Summary statistics compress information; they never replace looking at the histogram.

**Practice: run this example**

```
import pandas as pd

print(df.describe())                  # count, mean, std, min, quartiles, max
print(df["price"].skew())             # distribution shape
print(df.corr(numeric_only=True))     # correlation matrix

import pandas as pd
import numpy as np

income = np.array([30, 35, 40, 42, 45, 48, 50, 55, 60, 320])  # one billionaire skews it

s = pd.Series(income)

# 1) The core summary
print("mean :", s.mean().round(1))
print("median:", s.median())
print("std  :", s.std().round(1))

# 2) Why median is robust: the 320 outlier inflates the mean
print("mean-median gap:", round(s.mean() - s.median(), 1))

# 3) Quantiles + IQR + the classic outlier rule
q1, q3 = s.quantile([0.25, 0.75])
iqr = q3 - q1
print("IQR:", round(iqr, 1))
outliers = s[(s < q1 - 1.5 * iqr) | (s > q3 + 1.5 * iqr)]
print("outliers via 1.5*IQR rule:", list(outliers))

# 4) Skewness check -> log transform when > 1
print("skew:", round(s.skew(), 2))
log_income = np.log1p(income)
print("skew after log1p:", round(pd.Series(log_income).skew(), 2))

# 5) describe() gives the full univariate summary in one call
print(s.describe().round(2))
```
$ih$, 20, 14, 2, $ih$Data Cleaning, EDA & Feature Engineering$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$code$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)$ih$, $ih$EDA is investigating data before modeling, to catch problems and form hypotheses.

Reading these plots: a right-skewed histogram (long tail to the right) often signals income/price-type data and a candidate for a log transform. A box plot's whiskers show typical range; points beyond them are outlier candidates. A correlation heatmap near ±1 between two features signals redundancy (multicollinearity) — relevant again in Module 3.

**Why it matters.** EDA is where hypotheses are born: which features separate the classes, which columns are redundant, which distributions are dangerous for your chosen model. Skipping EDA is like going to court without reading the file — the data will surprise you in the worst possible moment. Analogy: EDA is reconnaissance before battle; it costs an hour and can prevent a week of modeling in the wrong direction.

**Step-by-step intuition.** Work in three layers: (1) Univariate — one column at a time: histogram for continuous (look for skew, modes, spikes at round numbers like 0 or 100 that hint at censoring), bar chart for categorical (watch for a dominant category that will fragment into a thousand sparse one-hot columns). (2) Bivariate — pairs: scatterplot for continuous vs continuous (shape hints at linear vs non-linear relationships), boxplot per category for continuous vs categorical (does the median separate the classes?), and the correlation matrix (|r| > 0.8 between two features = redundancy worth resolving; high correlation with the target = raw predictive material). (3) Multivariate — conditioned views like a scatter colored by class.

**Practitioner notes.** Correlation is not causation, but also not nothing: correlated features are redundant to models and often collinear enough to destabilize linear coefficients. Log every finding — a one-line note like "income strongly right-skewed, log-transformed in preprocessing" makes the notebook reviewable and the report defensible.

**Practice: run this example**

```
