  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Computer Vision$ih$, $ih$An image is a numeric array: height × width × channels (3 for RGB), each pixel an intensity value (0–255, usually rescaled to 0–1).

Image preprocessing includes resizing, normalization, and augmentation (flips/rotations to artificially expand training data).

CNNs (concept): Convolutional Neural Networks use small learnable filters that slide across an image (convolution) to detect local patterns like edges and textures, and pooling layers that downsample feature maps, keeping the strongest signals while reducing size — layered convolutions build up from simple edges to complex shapes.

Transfer learning (concept): instead of training a CNN from scratch, start from a model pretrained on a large dataset (e.g., ImageNet) and fine-tune it on your smaller dataset — dramatically reduces the data and compute needed for good performance.

**Why it matters.** Computer vision is the domain where deep learning visibly changed the world — self-driving perception, medical imaging, face recognition, and generative art all rest on CNN foundations. Understanding the CNN idea makes the field legible, and understanding representation (embeddings, pretrained feature extractors) makes it practical.

**Step-by-step intuition.** (1) Pixels as tensors: an image is a 3D array — height x width x channels (3 for RGB). A model that flattened it into a vector would lose spatial structure entirely. (2) The CNN trick: a small learnable filter (e.g., 3x3) slides across the image, producing a feature map that detects a local pattern (an edge, a corner) EVERYWHERE — this is translation invariance: a cat in the corner activates the same features as a cat in the center. (3) Stacked layers build hierarchy: early layers detect edges and color blobs, middle layers detect textures and parts, deep layers detect whole objects — a feature hierarchy learned, not designed. (4) Pooling downsamples feature maps, trading spatial precision for invariance and compute. (5) Practical reality: pretrained CNNs (ResNet, EfficientNet) are feature extractors out of the box — you chop off the classifier head, take the embedding, and train a small head on YOUR data (transfer learning), which works with far less data than training from scratch.

**Practitioner notes.** The professional defaults: start with a pretrained backbone + a small trainable head; resize and normalize images consistently; augment (flips, rotations, color jitter) when data is scarce. Training a CNN from scratch is reserved for research or huge datasets — in industry, transfer learning is the standard move.$ih$, 20, 80, 9, $ih$Applied ML: NLP & Computer Vision$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Classical ML vs. Deep Learning — When to Use Which$ih$, $ih$For small, structured/tabular datasets: classical ML (Modules 3-4) is usually faster, more interpretable, and just as accurate. For large, unstructured datasets (raw images, raw text, audio): deep learning typically wins, especially with transfer learning.

**Why it matters.** The classical-vs-deep decision is made daily on real projects, and the rule of thumb saves weeks: most tabular business problems are classical problems; most unstructured-data problems are deep problems. Analogy: a scalpel (classical) and a chainsaw (deep) are both cutting tools — the chainsaw is not "better", it is for a different tree.

**Step-by-step intuition.** Choose classical (linear models, trees, ensembles, gradient boosting) when: data is tabular, rows are independent-ish, samples are in the thousands-to-millions, interpretability matters, compute/time budgets are tight, or the team must debug in production. Choose deep learning when: inputs are unstructured (images, text, audio, video), there is abundant data (or strong pretrained models to adapt), the pattern is highly non-linear in raw space, or the state-of-the-art ceiling matters more than interpretability. The overlap zone is real — transformers beat boosted trees on some tabular benchmarks, and trees often serve as strong baselines in deep projects.

**Practitioner notes.** The professional workflow: always try the classical baseline first, even in deep projects — it defines the bar, often wins, and always helps debugging. Deep learning should EARN its complexity: it must beat the baseline by a meaningful margin to justify its cost in data, compute, and debugging.$ih$, 20, 81, 9, $ih$Applied ML: NLP & Computer Vision$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Responsible Data Handling$ih$, $ih$Dataset quality and bias: a text or image classifier trained on unrepresentative data will fail — or actively discriminate — on the populations it wasn't trained on. Always ask who is (and isn't) represented in your training data before trusting a model's outputs.

Evaluation limitations: offline metrics on a held-out set can miss real-world failure modes; always sanity-check model behavior on cases that matter most, not just aggregate scores.

**Why it matters.** NLP and CV data is data about people, and responsible handling is not a compliance add-on — it is a correctness requirement: biased data produces biased models, and privacy failures produce legal and reputational losses. Analogy: a pipeline that leaks customer data is like a plumber who uses sewage pipes for drinking water — technically "working", catastrophically wrong.

**Step-by-step intuition.** The practical checklist: (1) Privacy — text and images are personal data: anonymize (names, emails, faces), minimize collection, and respect consent; PII scrubbing is part of preprocessing, not an afterthought. (2) Bias — data collected under real-world conditions encodes historical patterns: a hiring model trained on past hires inherits past bias; audit your label distribution per demographic group and check for representation gaps before training. (3) Transparency — document data provenance, model limitations, and confidence bands; a disclaimer is not a fix, but silence is worse. (4) Governance — version and hash data, log who accessed what, and plan retention; the Module 7 reproducibility stack applies to sensitive data with extra force. (5) Fairness metrics — measure performance per group (demographic parity, equalized odds) when decisions affect people; report group-wise metrics alongside overall ones.

**Practitioner notes.** The professional attitude: responsible handling is the data engineering layer of this module. If a reviewer asks "what personal data does this pipeline hold, and why?", the answer should be a one-minute walkthrough of the data map — if it takes longer, the pipeline is not ready.$ih$, 20, 82, 9, $ih$Applied ML: NLP & Computer Vision$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Real-World Example$ih$, $ih$A spam filter trained mostly on English business email misclassifies casual, slang-heavy messages far more often — a direct consequence of dataset composition, not model architecture.

**Why it matters.** A real NLP/CV story demonstrates the module's themes in one arc — and shows why the "which model?" question was the smallest decision in the project.

**Step-by-step intuition.** The case: a support team building an email triage system that routes tickets to the right queue. (1) Data: 200k historical tickets with queue labels — noisy text, typos, mixed languages. (2) Baseline: TF-IDF + logistic regression hit 84% accuracy — already useful; error analysis (Module 5!) showed confusion between billing and account queues — ambiguous vocabulary, not model weakness. (3) Improvement: adding domain features (order numbers, product names) and a second label level lifted accuracy to 90%; the embedding-based deep model added 1% more for 10x the training cost — rejected on value. (4) Engineering: the vectorizer and model shipped as one pipeline artifact (Module 7), with class weights for rare queues (Module 4) and precision tuned at the threshold (Module 5). (5) Responsibility: the text held customer PII — scrubbed at ingestion, retained with governance, logged per the Module 9.6 checklist.

**Practitioner notes.** The lesson: the winning sequence was baseline → error analysis → targeted features → honest evaluation — with deep learning considered LAST and adopted only when it earned its cost. That is the professional pattern, and it recurs in every domain.$ih$, 20, 83, 9, $ih$Applied ML: NLP & Computer Vision$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Common Mistakes$ih$, $ih$- Not removing stopwords/normalizing text, letting frequency counts get dominated by uninformative words.
- Feeding raw un-normalized pixel values (0–255) into a network expecting 0–1, destabilizing training.
- Assuming a high overall accuracy means a vision/NLP model works equally well across all subgroups in the data.

**Why it matters.** NLP/CV amplify classic mistakes (leakage, imbalance) and add representation-specific ones — the list below covers the most expensive ones in practice.

**Step-by-step intuition.** (1) Fitting the vectorizer on all text pre-split — the vocabulary "seen" the test documents, leaking topic signal into training; fix: vectorizer inside the pipeline. (2) Treating raw TF-IDF features as meaningful — the model learns from text, but the coefficients are only interpretable in aggregate; reading individual weights as "the model hates this word" is folklore, not analysis. (3) Training CNNs from scratch with 2,000 images — guaranteed overfitting; fix: pretrained backbones and augmentation. (4) Ignoring class imbalance in text/image data — rare classes (a rare defect type, a rare language) vanish from the loss; fix: class weights and stratified splits. (5) Evaluating on the same distribution forever — a model trained on 2024 tickets drifts on 2026 tickets; fix: time-based splits, monitoring, and periodic refits. (6) Treating PII as optional preprocessing — a model that memorizes a name is a model that leaks it; fix: scrub before training, govern after.

**Practitioner notes.** The meta-habit: for every representation (bag-of-words, embeddings, pixel tensors), ask "what information is being thrown away, and what is being assumed?" — bag-of-words throws away order; embeddings assume semantic proximity; CNNs assume spatial locality. Knowing the assumption is knowing the failure mode.$ih$, 20, 84, 9, $ih$Applied ML: NLP & Computer Vision$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Professional Tip$ih$, $ih$For any new NLP or vision problem, start with the simplest viable baseline (TF-IDF + logistic regression; or a small pretrained CNN via transfer learning) before reaching for a large custom architecture — it's often shockingly competitive and much cheaper to build and maintain.

**Why it matters.** The professional tip for applied NLP/CV: adapt, evaluate, and document — the three practices that turn a demo model into a maintained system.

**Step-by-step intuition.** (1) Adapt, do not reinvent: pretrained embeddings, feature extractors, and large models are the professional starting points — fine-tuning a small head on your data is faster, cheaper, and usually better than training from scratch. (2) Evaluate in context: report performance on the slices that matter (per class, per demographic group, per time period), not just the global number — the global number hides the failures. (3) Document the assumptions: what language(s), what image domains, what class ratios the model was built for — because every model carries the biases of its training distribution, and documentation is the only way consumers can know.

**Practitioner notes.** The professional standard for any NLP/CV deliverable: baseline metrics, slice metrics, an error analysis summary, and a model card (intended use, limitations, training data). That one page is what separates "we built a model" from "we built a trustworthy system".$ih$, 20, 85, 9, $ih$Applied ML: NLP & Computer Vision$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Introduction$ih$, $ih$This final module ties everything together: framing a business problem as an ML problem, understanding deployment at the level an ML engineer needs, and preparing you to plan and document a real capstone project.

**Why it matters.** This module is the destination of the whole course: turning a trained model into a running, monitored, valuable system. Deployment is where ML projects die in the real world — studies consistently show most models never reach production, and the failures are rarely algorithmic. Analogy: a race car that wins every test lap but cannot start on race day is not a race car; a model that wins every benchmark but cannot serve traffic is not a product.

**Step-by-step intuition.** The module's arc: (1) problem framing — converting a business need into a measurable ML objective; (2) model selection and honest evaluation — the Module 5 discipline applied at system level; (3) serving — APIs, batch pipelines, and the engineering that keeps a model alive in production; (4) the capstone — your chance to build all of it end-to-end. Every earlier module reappears here as a production decision.

**Practitioner notes.** Adopt the system mindset now: from the first line of the capstone, ask "how would this run 24/7, how would it fail, and how would I know?" — that single habit converts a course project into a portfolio piece that engineers recognize as production-shaped.$ih$, 20, 86, 10, $ih$End-to-End ML Systems, Deployment & Professional Capstone$ih$, true, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Learning Objectives$ih$, $ih$- Translate a business problem into a well-defined ML problem.
- Define success metrics, baselines, and evaluate model limitations honestly.
- Explain deployment concepts: inference modes, APIs, monitoring, drift.
- Plan and document a complete, professional ML project.

**Why it matters.** The objectives define the end-of-course capability — the professional trifecta of framing, evaluation, and deployment that caps the curriculum.

**Step-by-step intuition.** Map each objective: "convert a business problem into an ML problem" means you can translate "reduce customer churn" into a defined target, a metric, a feasibility check, and a decision threshold — before any code runs. "Select and evaluate models in context" means you choose candidates by the problem's error costs and validate with the Module 5 apparatus, reporting the honest generalization estimate. "Explain deployment concepts" means you can discuss serving patterns (batch vs. online), monitoring, drift, and rollback with enough depth to design a small system. "Design a capstone" means you can scope, build, and document a complete project: data → pipeline → model → artifact → serving → evaluation report. "Practice reproducibility" means the project is a scripted, seeded, documented system, not a notebook.

**Practitioner notes.** The capstone is evaluated like a work sample: reviewers look for the engineering layer — structure (Module 7), honest evaluation (Module 5), serving (Module 10), and documentation — more than for model score. A clean 85% pipeline beats a messy 96% notebook every time.$ih$, 20, 87, 10, $ih$End-to-End ML Systems, Deployment & Professional Capstone$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$From Business Problem to ML Problem$ih$, $ih$"We have a business problem" (e.g., "too many customers are leaving") becomes "we have an ML problem" only once you define:

- The target: what exactly are we predicting — churn in the next 30 days?
- The features: what's available before the outcome, without leakage.
- The success metric: what number defines "good enough," tied to business cost — e.g., recall above X%.
- The dataset requirements: enough labeled history, representative of future conditions.
- A baseline: what's the current, non-ML approach's performance, if any?

**Why it matters.** Framing is the most undervalued step in applied ML: most project failures are framing failures — the wrong target, the wrong metric, or a problem that never needed ML at all. Analogy: a contractor who starts pouring concrete before checking whether the client needs a house or a boat has made an expensive error that no amount of concrete will fix.

**Step-by-step intuition.** The framing checklist: (1) The business question — "reduce churn by 10% this quarter" — is not the ML question; the ML question is "predict which customers will cancel in the next 30 days, so the retention team can act". (2) The target must be measurable and causal-adjacent: churn needs a time window and a definition ("no purchase for 90 days"), and the target must not leak the decision you want to inform. (3) The metric must match the error costs: churn with expensive false negatives wants recall-oriented metrics at a tuned threshold; the dollars decide, not the default 0.5. (4) Feasibility: is there signal? — the baseline check from Module 5: if the simple baseline cannot beat the majority-class rule, reframe or abandon. (5) The decision loop: what action happens on the prediction, who takes it, and how is model value measured — a model nobody acts on has no value.

**Practitioner notes.** The professional habit: write the one-paragraph framing statement before any code — business problem, ML formulation, target definition, metric, threshold logic, and action. Projects with a written framing statement ship; projects without one drift.$ih$, 20, 88, 10, $ih$End-to-End ML Systems, Deployment & Professional Capstone$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Model Selection, Evaluation & Limitations$ih$, $ih$Choosing a model is a tradeoff between accuracy, interpretability, latency, and maintenance cost — the "best" model on a leaderboard isn't always the best choice for production.

Error analysis (Module 5) on the final model, and an explicit, written list of model limitations (what data it hasn't seen, what populations it may underperform on, what it should not be used for) are professional obligations, not optional extras.

**Why it matters.** Model selection at system level is about the whole lifecycle — not just accuracy, but latency, interpretability, maintenance cost, and failure modes. The best-scoring model is frequently not the best model to deploy. Analogy: choosing a car by top speed alone ignores fuel, cargo space, and maintenance — the same car, different context, different winner.

**Step-by-step intuition.** The system-level selection matrix: (1) Performance — honest CV estimate plus slice metrics (per class, per segment), not a single global number. (2) Operational cost — inference latency and memory at production traffic; a 0.1% accuracy gain from a model 20x slower is usually a bad trade. (3) Interpretability — regulated domains (credit, healthcare) may require explainable models or explanation tools (SHAP/LIME) even at an accuracy cost. (4) Maintenance — every model drifts; simpler models are cheaper to retrain, re-validate, and debug. (5) Failure modes — what does the model do on out-of-distribution input, and how does the system catch it? Calibration curves and confidence thresholds matter. The decision is a weighted trade across these axes, made with stakeholders, not in isolation.

**Practitioner notes.** The professional output is a selection memo: candidates, their scores on each axis, and the decision with reasons. Writing the memo forces the trade-offs into the open — and the memo survives to be revisited when the model drifts, which it will.$ih$, 20, 89, 10, $ih$End-to-End ML Systems, Deployment & Professional Capstone$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Deployment Concepts$ih$, $ih$- Inference: using a trained, saved model (Module 7) to produce predictions.
- Model artifacts: the serialized model plus its exact preprocessing pipeline and input/output contract.
- Batch inference: scoring many records at once on a schedule (e.g., nightly churn scores for all customers).
- Real-time inference: scoring one request at a time, on demand, typically behind an API.
- API concepts: a deployed model is usually wrapped in an endpoint that accepts structured input, validates it (input validation), and returns predictions in a defined format (output contract).
- Monitoring: tracking prediction distributions, latency, and — where ground truth becomes available — actual accuracy over time in production.
- Model drift / data drift: performance degrades over time because the real world changes — either the input data's distribution shifts (data drift) or the relationship between inputs and outputs shifts (model/concept drift). Retraining on fresh data, on a schedule or when drift is detected, is the standard response.

**Why it matters.** Deployment is where engineering reality meets the model: serving patterns, drift, and rollback define the difference between "a model" and "a service". Understanding the concepts makes you fluent in the production conversation — and the capstone will ask you to build a small slice of it. Analogy: the training notebook is the test kitchen; deployment is the restaurant floor — timing, reliability, and recovery are the real business.

**Step-by-step intuition.** (1) Serving patterns: batch inference (scores recomputed nightly on the day's data — simple, cheap, right for churn scoring) vs. online inference (a trained model behind an API answering individual requests in milliseconds — right for fraud screening at transaction time). (2) The serving stack: the serialized artifact from Module 7 wrapped in a thin service (e.g., FastAPI) that loads it once and exposes a predict endpoint — plus the golden rule: preprocessing lives INSIDE the served pipeline, so raw requests hit the same transforms the model saw in training. (3) Monitoring: performance decays without a model changing — data drift (inputs shift: new customer types), concept drift (the relationship shifts: pandemic changes churn patterns), and prediction drift (output distribution moves); the defense is monitoring distributions (PSI/KL), alerting on drift thresholds, and periodic retraining. (4) Rollback: versioned artifacts and shadow/blue-green deploys mean a bad model can be reverted in minutes — the Module 7 versioning discipline is the rollback mechanism.

**Practitioner notes.** The professional minimal production setup: artifact + serving script + input schema validation + output logging + drift monitor + rollback path. You can build all six in a weekend — and the capstone is the excuse to do it.

**Practice: run this example**

```
# Real-time endpoint payload & contract validation
def predict(payload):
    # validate the incoming request against the model's input contract
    validated_data = validate_schema(payload)
    return model.predict(validated_data)

# A minimal, production-shaped serving script (FastAPI pattern)
# Saved artifact from 7.5 is loaded ONCE; requests flow through the full pipeline.

# from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException
# from pydantic import BaseModel
# import joblib
