#     # Schema validation rejects bad input BEFORE the model sees it
#     if req.age < 0 or req.income < 0:
#         raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail="negative feature values")
#     row = [[req.age, req.income, req.months_active]]
#     proba = float(model.predict_proba(row)[0, 1])
#     return {"churn_probability": round(proba, 4)}
#
# Deployment checklist the code above satisfies:
#  1) Artifact = full pipeline (preprocessing included)      [Module 7]
#  2) Input schema validated at the boundary                  [robustness]
#  3) One endpoint, one well-defined response                 [simplicity]
#  4) Log every request + response for drift monitoring       [Module 10]
#  5) Artifact versioned -> instant rollback on regression    [Module 7]

# Monitoring, in one paragraph of math:
#   data drift:   P_serving(x) != P_train(x)  -> watch PSI/KL per feature
#   concept drift: P(y | x) changed over time -> watch live vs. holdout accuracy
#   remedy loop:  alert -> audit -> retrain on fresh labeled data -> rollback if worse
```
$ih$, 20, 90, 10, $ih$End-to-End ML Systems, Deployment & Professional Capstone$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$code$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Real-World Example$ih$, $ih$A retail demand-forecasting model, deployed with no monitoring, silently degrades over eight months as shopping habits shift post-launch — a textbook case of undetected data drift, and the reason production ML systems need monitoring, not just a one-time evaluation.

**Why it matters.** The final real-world example ties the entire course into one system story — the complete lifecycle of a production ML system, with the failures and fixes that made it real.

**Step-by-step intuition.** The case: a bank's loan-default model, followed for three years. (1) Framing (10.3): "reduce defaults without over-rejecting good customers" became: predict P(default in 24 months), with the threshold chosen by the two cost types. (2) Evaluation (10.4): logistic baseline vs. gradient-boosted candidate — the boosted model won on AUC but the bank shipped the logistic model for interpretability in a regulated setting, with SHAP explanations on top. (3) Serving (10.5): nightly batch scoring, artifact + schema validation + logging. (4) The drift story: two years in, PSI alarms fire — the product mix changed (new loan products); the retrain-and-rollback loop from 10.5 executed; the model recovered. (5) The audit: every prediction traceable to a versioned artifact, data hash, and commit — the Module 7 discipline paying off during a regulatory review. (6) Responsibility (9.6): group-wise metrics reported quarterly; the model's demographic-parity gap measured and documented.

**Practitioner notes.** The lesson: the model was the smallest part of the system. Framing, honest evaluation, monitoring, and governance carried the project; the algorithm was replaceable, the process was not. That is the course's thesis in one story.$ih$, 20, 91, 10, $ih$End-to-End ML Systems, Deployment & Professional Capstone$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Common Mistakes$ih$, $ih$- Deploying a model with no monitoring or retraining plan, treating "shipped" as "finished."
- Skipping input validation, letting malformed or out-of-range data silently produce nonsense predictions.
- Presenting only aggregate metrics to stakeholders without discussing the model's known limitations.

**Why it matters.** These end-of-course mistakes are the professional failure modes — each is the kind of thing that separates "ML project" from "ML system".

**Step-by-step intuition.** (1) Shipping without a baseline comparison — no framing, no "what were we beating?" story; the model's value cannot be stated. (2) Optimizing the wrong metric — global accuracy on imbalanced data, or latency-blind accuracy on a real-time service. (3) Deploying a model with no monitoring — drift arrives silently, predictions degrade for months before anyone notices. (4) Skipping rollback planning — the first bad deploy takes days to undo instead of minutes. (5) Ignoring input validation — garbage requests produce confident garbage scores, and nobody flags it. (6) Training on the full dataset "now that we are shipping" — the final retrain without a validation story means the reported number was never the shipped number.

**Practitioner notes.** The course's last rule: every shipped model needs a one-page operations doc — model card, expected performance, drift thresholds, alert contacts, and rollback steps. If the doc does not exist, the deployment is not finished, no matter how well the model scored.$ih$, 20, 92, 10, $ih$End-to-End ML Systems, Deployment & Professional Capstone$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, ''),
  ($ih$00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001$ih$, $ih$Professional Tip$ih$, $ih$Document your capstone (and any real project) as if handing it to a teammate who has never seen it: problem definition, data, approach, results, limitations, and how to reproduce it. This document is often more valuable, long-term, than the model file itself.

**Why it matters.** The final professional tip is the course's closing message: completeness over cleverness. The capstone, and every project after it, is judged as a system — and the system habits are the deliverable this course was built to produce.

**Step-by-step intuition.** The professional's capstone checklist — which is also the hiring-interview checklist: (1) framing statement (10.3) written first; (2) data profile and quality audit (Module 2); (3) EDA with documented findings (Module 2); (4) preprocessing + modeling inside pipelines (Modules 3-5); (5) honest evaluation with CV and baselines (Module 5); (6) artifact with metadata (Module 7); (7) serving script with schema validation (Module 10); (8) monitoring plan and drift thresholds (Module 10); (9) reproducibility: one command rebuilds everything (Module 7); (10) a README that tells the whole story — problem, approach, results, limitations.

**Practitioner notes.** The final professional habit: when you finish any project, re-read your own work as a stranger would, and ask "could this person rebuild, understand, and trust this system with zero help?" — if the answer is no, finish the documentation, not the model. That is the standard the capstone will be held to, and it is the standard the industry holds professionals to.$ih$, 20, 93, 10, $ih$End-to-End ML Systems, Deployment & Professional Capstone$ih$, false, false, true, $ih$theory$ih$, '');
